1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:01,468
This time you’re the reviewer.

2
00:00:01,730 --> 00:00:07,255
In the approved remote terminal, confirm
that GitHub CLI is using your own

3
00:00:07,255 --> 00:00:10,516
authorized account and that you are in
the training repository.

4
00:00:10,837 --> 00:00:14,256
The author sends you pull request
forty-two. Open that request for this

5
00:00:14,256 --> 00:00:14,904
exercise.

6
00:00:15,285 --> 00:00:17,800
We’ll check the intent first, then the
actual patch,

7
00:00:18,051 --> 00:00:20,345
and finally the checks for the revision
you’re reviewing.

8
00:00:21,297 --> 00:00:25,605
Run the GitHub CLI view command for pull
request forty-two.

9
00:00:26,016 --> 00:00:30,464
Read the title and the issue link. The
author says zero should be rejected and

10
00:00:30,464 --> 00:00:32,204
all four unit tests now pass.

11
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:34,117
That tells you what to look for.

12
00:00:34,378 --> 00:00:37,886
Keep the issue open beside the terminal
so you can compare the requested

13
00:00:37,886 --> 00:00:40,370
behavior with the change you’re about to
inspect.

14
00:00:41,338 --> 00:00:45,186
Now view the actual diff. The main
change removes equality at the boundary.

15
00:00:45,447 --> 00:00:48,797
The second change adds a test that calls
the validator with zero.

16
00:00:49,159 --> 00:00:51,868
Ask yourself whether that test would
fail on the old code.

17
00:00:52,285 --> 00:00:56,246
This is the difference between seeing a
test name and seeing a test that proves

18
00:00:56,246 --> 00:00:57,244
the relevant behavior.

19
00:00:58,213 --> 00:01:01,632
Look up the pull request’s required
checks. In the training example,

20
00:01:01,863 --> 00:01:03,698
the unit test check is required.

21
00:01:04,148 --> 00:01:07,307
Open its run if you need to confirm
which suite it executed.

22
00:01:07,778 --> 00:01:09,984
If the checks are pending, wait for the
result.

23
00:01:10,496 --> 00:01:14,418
If the author pushes again, refresh the
diff and checks for the new head before

24
00:01:14,418 --> 00:01:15,528
making your decision.

25
00:01:16,387 --> 00:01:18,835
Here’s the alternate outcome. The checks
are green,

26
00:01:19,085 --> 00:01:22,884
but the test added in this version
exercises one and never calls the

27
00:01:22,884 --> 00:01:24,000
validator with zero.

28
00:01:24,461 --> 00:01:28,651
That leaves the bug untested. Use the
request-changes option and ask for the

29
00:01:28,651 --> 00:01:29,408
missing assertion.

30
00:01:29,854 --> 00:01:34,430
Give the author a specific behavior to
verify. Review the new revision after

31
00:01:34,430 --> 00:01:35,098
they address it.

32
00:01:36,047 --> 00:01:39,566
Return to the version with the right
condition and the actual zero test.

33
00:01:39,967 --> 00:01:42,180
If you’ve checked the diff and the
required results,

34
00:01:42,611 --> 00:01:45,344
you can approve with a short statement
of what you verified.

35
00:01:45,705 --> 00:01:49,225
Submit the review from your account,
then read back the reviews on the pull

36
00:01:49,225 --> 00:01:49,671
request.

37
00:01:50,053 --> 00:01:52,839
Confirm your decision was saved to the
intended request.

38
00:01:53,803 --> 00:01:57,918
Pair up and swap author and reviewer
roles. Give each reviewer an issue,

39
00:01:58,109 --> 00:02:00,103
a pull request, and the check results.

40
00:02:00,484 --> 00:02:03,464
In one run, the evidence is complete. In
the other,

41
00:02:03,636 --> 00:02:05,432
leave out the test for the key input.

42
00:02:06,003 --> 00:02:08,873
Ask the reviewer to explain their
decision in one sentence.

43
00:02:09,384 --> 00:02:12,647
It should tell the author what was
verified or what needs to change.
